/*!
    \previouspage Plugin
    \nextpage QtApplication
    \qmltype QmlModule
    \inherits CppLibrary
    \inqmlmodule QbsConvenienceItems
    \ingroup list-of-items
    \keyword QML.QmlModule

    \brief QML extension module consisting of a plugin library and QML files.

    A QmlModule is a convenience item that mirrors the functionality of
    CMake's \c qt_add_qml_module() command. It combines a plugin library with
    automatic QML type registration, qmldir generation, and resource embedding,
    so that the module can be imported from QML using the module-based import syntax
    (rather than file or directory imports).

    \note This functionality requires Qt 6.2 or later.

    \section2 What QmlModule Does Automatically

    \list
    \li Runs \c qmltyperegistrar on C++ files decorated with \c QML_ELEMENT or
        similar macros and produces a \c plugins.qmltypes file.
    \li Generates a \c qmldir file listing all QML files and, if
        \l{Qt.qml::loadedAtRuntime}{Qt.qml.loadedAtRuntime} is \c true, a
        \c plugin line for dynamic loading.
    \li Embeds all QML files, JavaScript files, and the \c qmldir into the
        plugin's resources at \c{:/qt/qml/<URI>/}, enabling the
        \c{prefer :/qt/qml/<URI>/} directive so the QML engine uses embedded
        resources rather than files on disk.
    \li Installs \c qmldir, \c plugins.qmltypes, as well as QML and JavaScript files to
        \l modulesInstallDir. When the plugin is loaded at runtime
        (\l{Qt.qml::loadedAtRuntime}{Qt.qml.loadedAtRuntime} is \c true),
        the library binary is installed there too.
    \endlist

    \section2 Usage

    \section3 Dynamic Plugin — Linked Directly by the Application

    The application lists the module as a dependency, which links the plugin at
    build time. The QML engine finds module types through the pre-registered
    module without loading a plugin file at runtime. Because
    \l{Qt.qml::loadedAtRuntime}{Qt.qml.loadedAtRuntime} defaults to \c true for
    dynamic libraries, set it to \c false to suppress the \c plugin line in the
    generated \c qmldir.

    \code
    // mymodule/mymodule.qbs
    QmlModule {
        name: "mymoduleplugin"
        uri: "com.example.MyModule"
        Qt.qml.loadedAtRuntime: false
        files: ["MyItem.qml", "mytype.h", "mytype.cpp"]
    }

    // app/app.qbs
    CppApplication {
        Depends { name: "mymoduleplugin" }
        // ...
    }
    \endcode

    \section3 Dynamic Plugin — Loaded at Runtime via Import Path

    In this mode the plugin \c .so is not linked by the application. Instead,
    the QML engine discovers and loads it at runtime through the QML import
    path. \l{Qt.qml::loadedAtRuntime}{Qt.qml.loadedAtRuntime} defaults to
    \c true for dynamic libraries, so no override is needed:

    \code
    QmlModule {
        name: "mymoduleplugin"
        uri: "com.example.MyModule"
        files: [...]
    }
    \endcode

    However, you need to point the QML engine to the install location via
    \c QML_IMPORT_PATH or \c{QQmlEngine::addImportPath()}.

    \section3 Static Plugin

    For platforms that require static linking (iOS, tvOS, watchOS), the type is
    automatically set to \c staticlibrary. On other platforms, override
    \l{Product::type}{type} directly. \l{Qt.qml::loadedAtRuntime}{Qt.qml.loadedAtRuntime}
    defaults to \c false for static libraries, so no override is needed there
    either.
    \code
    QmlModule {
        name: "mymoduleplugin"
        uri: "com.example.MyModule"
        type: ["staticlibrary"]
        files: [...]
    }

    CppApplication {
        Depends { name: "mymoduleplugin" }
        // ...
    }
    \endcode

    \since Qbs 3.4
*/

/*!
    \qmlproperty string QmlModule::uri

    The QML module URI, for example \c{"com.example.MyModule"}. This value is
    required.

    The URI is used as the \l{Qt.qml::importName}{Qt.qml.importName} for type
    registration, written as the \c module line in the generated \c qmldir, and
    determines the resource path \c{:/qt/qml/<URI>/} where QML files and the
    \c qmldir are embedded.

    \nodefaultvalue
*/

/*!
    \qmlproperty string QmlModule::modulesInstallDir

    The directory where \c qmldir, \c plugins.qmltypes, and QML files are
    installed. When \l{Qt.qml::loadedAtRuntime}{Qt.qml.loadedAtRuntime} is
    \c true, the plugin library is also installed here so the QML engine can
    find it.

    \defaultvalue \c{Qt.qml.qmlPath + "/" + uri.replace(/\./g, "/")}
*/

